title: Auditing by duplicated predicate
tags: Development
kind: guide
audience: dev
summary: On a mature code base, linear re-reading finds nothing; looking for a question the code answers in two places, and comparing the two answers, found five bugs.

On a code base that is already clean — clippy at `-D warnings`, the invariants
checked, differential oracles everywhere — reading the code from top to bottom
returns nothing. What does: ''find a question the code answers in two places, and
compare the two answers.''

When one implementation states its rule ("a broken symlink still counts") and
another does the opposite three files away, that is a bug, with no expected
behaviour to guess. It is the logic of the oracles ([[Testing conventions]]),
applied by reading and without a harness. The audit of September 2026 found five
that way, among them the broken symbolic link ([[Symbolic links]]).

!! Pairs that paid

* the orphan scan of `orphans.rs` against the one in `reconcile.rs`;
* `copy_tree` against `copy_path`, in the same file;
* the daemon against the GUI on "does this file exist?";
* `path_of` (daemon, the root is `""`) against `treeRefPath` (JavaScript, the root was `"/"`) — [[TreeRef path conventions]].

!! Pairs checked, with nothing to find

`reconcile::walk` against the watcher's `compute_watched_dirs` (one benign
difference); the three JavaScript copies of `dslString`; `escapeRegex` against the
metacharacters of the `regex` crate.

!! Angles that returned nothing

`TODO` and `FIXME` (none in the sources), `let _ =` (all legitimate), `.unwrap()`
(nearly all in tests), endpoints without a test (none), copy-paste detection (only in
tests, on purpose).

The other method that pays on this code base is to measure rather than read
([[Storage pitfalls]], [[Performance testing]]). To find a cost paid once per
element, grep for a costly call inside a loop, then ''count'' it with a stub — a
`DaemonClient` that counts requests needs neither a daemon nor a large repository.
